  "editorial_note": "GitHub listed eleven projects this Wednesday morning. Eight had been featured before, six of them within the past week, and none of those six showed a change big enough to bring it back, so today's edition has three picks. DeepTutor leads as ==a self-hosted AI tutoring workspace== built around your own documents. AI Design Skills, GitHub's top entry today, is a single readable landing-page skill for coding agents, and Superpowers returns after eleven days because its 7.0.1 release changed how it plans work before building. The remaining new entry, a collection of model jailbreak prompts, was left out. Curation order reflects practical interest, not GitHub rank. Trending is not a release date or an endorsement.",
  "coverage_notes": [
    "All 11 listing entries were inspected. Exact primary public GitHub repository metadata and recent releases were checked for all 11 at about 07:01 UTC, and README files for the three picks and the other new entries; all were active and unarchived. Ten had an API-detected license; togg53192-cmd/jailbreaks had none.",
    "Six entries featured in the previous seven days were left out without a material change: nightly-labs/openbot and gitroomhq/postiz-app (27 October), JetBrains/thinkrail (26 October), Nutlope/hallmark, pbakaus/impeccable and makecindy/cindy (25 October). Since those cards they show patch releases, nightly or beta builds, new themes or no new release. Impeccable's skill 4.8.0 of 26 October now checks engine downloads against digests committed with the skill, which narrows one caveat on our card.",
    "affaan-m/ECC (featured 16 October) was not repeated: its latest release, v2.2.3 of 1 October, predates that card. obra/superpowers (featured 17 October) returns because of v7.0.1, released on 21 October, after that card.",
    "togg53192-cmd/jailbreaks, a collection of prompts written to override model safety policies, was not picked: GitHub detects no license, and its purpose is to defeat safeguards rather than to serve as a practical tool.",
    "The preceding seven calendar dates, 21-27 October, were checked for local history; all seven editions exist.",
    "The official listing was captured at 08:00:34 Europe/Paris. Literal stars-today labels are GitHub's displayed period figures; API total stars and README evidence were checked separately at the times shown per card. They are not an independently audited rolling 24-hour growth measurement.",
    "Feature, performance and quality claims are the maintainers' own, including Superpowers' test-scenario figures. No candidate code, installer, skill, plugin, setup prompt or model was installed, downloaded or run, and none of this is a security audit or endorsement."
  ],
  "projects": [
    {
      "repository": "HKUDS/DeepTutor",
      "url": "https://github.com/HKUDS/DeepTutor",
      "trending_rank": 9,
      "description": "An ==agent-native learning workspace== you run yourself: chat tutoring, quizzes, guided Mastery Paths, research, visualisations, course study and immersive reading of PDFs, EPUBs and YouTube or Bilibili videos, grounded in knowledge bases built from your own documents, GitHub repositories, documentation sites or an Obsidian vault. It runs as a local web app and CLI, installed from PyPI, from source or with Docker, using the model providers you configure.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. Most AI study tools are hosted chat windows; this keeps your materials, progress and ==inspectable, editable tutoring memory== in a workspace you control, and it checks understanding rather than only answering. Version 1.6.15 (18 October) added independently graded question groups, grading that separates incomplete from incorrect answers, and bounded memory of a learner's unresolved misunderstandings. A CLI with machine-readable output lets other agents drive it, and the design is described in an arXiv preprint.",
      "caveat": "A large, fast-moving system that needs Python 3.11-3.14 plus Node.js 20 or later, or Docker, and your own LLM, embedding and search providers, whose usage you pay for; the release notes say memory compression has its own model cost. The README's quick start suggests ==pasting a setup prompt into a coding agent==, which lets that agent act on your machine, and its file sandbox is only best effort without Docker or a system sandbox. Teaching-quality claims are the maintainers'. We did not install or test it.",
      "category": "Education / AI agents",
      "language": "Python",
      "license": "Apache-2.0",
      "stars_total": 46603,
      "stars_period": 1598,
      "stars_period_label": "1,598 stars today",
